## GNAudio

**Component:** `libasound_module_pcm_gamenative.so` and the audio engine it contains
**Location:** `app/src/main/assets/gamenative-audio-guest.tzst`, installed at runtime
into the container image as `usr/lib/alsa-lib/libasound_module_pcm_gamenative.so` and
`usr/share/alsa/gamenative.conf`
**Source:** https://github.com/GameNative/gamenative-audio/tree/fae5e10ab9ca3983d982de4d0f89fe451577049e
**License:** LGPL-2.1-or-later (GNU Lesser General Public License v2.1 or later)
**Copyright:** (C) 2026 GameNative

The payload is packed from the linked revision by `scripts/pack-alsa-asset.sh`,
which rebuilds the plugin from source and writes the archive; running it reproduces the
shipped payload from the same revision.
The bundled archive is the artifact published in
https://github.com/utkarshdalal/GameNative/pull/2476/commits/682ba12cb92d70bf6678a1c9f5ebe8bad6b3750e.
Its SHA-256 is `c31153e6ddbfc14503d973a37917ee73629996553b7798d37fd9a5b0600877d8`.

GNAudio is GameNative's audio engine and its ALSA plugin, written in Rust. It is a single shared object: the engine is a static dependency of the plugin, so
there is nothing else to install, and no Wine-side driver, daemon, socket or helper
process is involved. It is loaded by the container's libasound when a game plays audio,
and delivers the stream to Android through `AAudio`.

It is offered under the LGPL-2.1, and it is reached through a public, documented
interface: libasound's ioplug PCM contract. The shared object is a separate file in the
container image, loaded at runtime and never linked against the application or against
Wine, which is the arrangement LGPL-2.1 §6 (b) describes. The application itself is
GPL-3.0; the plugin keeps its own LGPL-2.1 terms.

### Replacing the library

The plugin is a standalone file. Anyone may replace it with their own build of the
same source, or with a modified version, provided the replacement exports the entry
point libasound looks up by name (`_snd_pcm_gamenative_open`) and is placed at the path
above. Gameplay keeps an installed replacement until the bundled archive changes or
the container image is rebuilt. The replacement need not match the bundled binary's
checksum. Reverse engineering of the library for the purpose of debugging such
modifications is permitted, as LGPL-2.1 §6 requires.

### Third-party code

The engine is original work with no third-party code in it and no dependency beyond
the platform: the AAudio interface is declared by hand against the NDK's own header,
and the C ABI in `include/gnaudio.h` is this project's own.

One exception, and it is headers rather than code: the plugin vendors alsa-lib 1.2.13's
public headers at `alsa-plugin/include/alsa/` (32 headers plus a `sound/` subset), which
are LGPL-2.1-or-later and carry their own copyright notices. Every vendored header that
exists in that release is byte-identical to it; the two alsa-lib generates at configure
time (`asoundlib.h`, `version.h`) are as 1.2.13 produces them. They are the same license
as this component, so the terms above cover them unchanged. The ALSA plugin SDK those
headers define — the exported entry point's name and the `snd_pcm_ioplug_t` layout
libasound fills — is libasound's interface, not this project's design.

